Following up on the three memory-leak comments with the piece I should have led with, plus a measurement. Correcting the measurement I posted first, which did not support what I said it did.

**Who deletes the builder.** `tensorflow/c/kernels.cc:298`:

```c++
class KernelBuilderFactory
    : public ::tensorflow::kernel_factory::OpKernelFactory {
 public:
  explicit KernelBuilderFactory(TF_KernelBuilder* builder)
      : builder_(builder) {}
  ...
  ~KernelBuilderFactory() override { TF_DeleteKernelBuilder(builder_); }
 private:
  TF_KernelBuilder* builder_;
};
```

and `TF_DeleteKernelBuilder` at `:122` deletes both `cc_builder` and the struct. So the factory owns the builder and frees it. `TF_RegisterKernelBuilder` hands the builder to that factory and sets the status to OK unconditionally, because with no serialized `KernelDef` there is nothing to fail on:

```c++
  if (serialized_kernel_def == nullptr) {
    tensorflow::kernel_factory::OpKernelRegistrar(
        builder->cc_builder->Build(), name,
        std::make_unique<tensorflow::KernelBuilderFactory>(builder));
    TF_SetStatus(status, TF_OK, "");
    return;
  }
```

The one non-OK path in that function returns before constructing the factory, and it is unreachable from here since we never pass a serialized `KernelDef`. The scenario the comments describe, a call to `TF_RegisterKernelBuilder` that fails, therefore cannot arise on this path; and deleting the builder after a call that returned would be a double free, since the factory frees it when it is destroyed.

**Measured.** A standalone program that registers 356 builders through the same helper shape as the kernels here, under `leaks`:

```
registered 356 builders
Process 31516: 6117 nodes malloced for 502 KB
Process 31516: 0 leaks for 0 total leaked bytes.
```

I first posted a measurement taken on the Python interpreter instead, which reported one 32-byte leak and no mention of `TF_KernelBuilder`. That was not evidence: the report carried the line "leaks can only show or save contents of readonly memory of restricted processes", so the absence of those symbols said only that the tool was not allowed to show block contents. The run above is on an unhardened binary and does support the claim.

Kept the code as it is, and pushed a comment at each site saying who owns the builder after the call, since three separate comments landed on the same five lines and that is a fair sign the code did not explain itself.

### 1. Native CPU Kernel Zero Initialization (`tensorflow/core/kernels/maxpooling_op.cc`)
While `MklMaxPoolingGradOp` in `mkl_maxpooling_op.cc` is now correctly updated to allocate output with `orig_input_tensor.shape()` and zero it, the native CPU kernel path in `tensorflow/core/kernels/maxpooling_op.cc` also needs to ensure `input_backprop` is zeroed out when the forward output is empty.

In `SpatialMaxPoolWithArgMaxHelper` (`tensorflow/core/kernels/maxpooling_op.cc`), when `output->NumElements() == 0` (e.g. when valid padding with a window larger than input dimension produces an empty output), the function returns early without running the backprop initialization (`in_shard.setConstant(T(0));`), leaving newly allocated `input_backprop` with uninitialized memory.

Please update `SpatialMaxPoolWithArgMaxHelper` in `tensorflow/core/kernels/maxpooling_op.cc` to zero out `input_backprop` on early return:

```cpp
  if (tensor_in.NumElements() == 0 || output->NumElements() == 0) {
    if (input_backprop != nullptr) {
      input_backprop->flat<T>().setZero();
    }
    return;
  }
```

